Why Entry Point Analysis Matters for Phoenix
Every protective measure in Phoenix traces back to a specific entry point risk. Data Validation exists because of Entry Points 1 and 2. Multi-factor authentication exists because of Entry Point 3. The Discard if Irrelevant filter exists because of Entry Points 4 and 5. TLS 1.3 encryption and OAuth 2.0 authentication exist because of Entry Point 6. Phoenix reaches out into the world to protect it — and that reach is both its greatest strength and its greatest exposure. Identifying and understanding every entry point is what ensures that openness never becomes a vulnerability (Chakraborty et al., 2024; Azambuja et al., 2023).

Identifying Trust Boundaries
If entry points tell us where data and users enter Phoenix, trust boundaries tell us where the system stops and asks — should I actually trust this? A trust boundary is a deliberate line drawn within the architecture where data or control passes from one zone to another, and where Phoenix must make a conscious decision about whether what is crossing that line has earned the right to proceed.
In a system like Phoenix, trust is not given freely. A validated government API feed is not the same as an anonymous community report. An authenticated DMT administrator action is not the same as a public submission. Recognising where these differences matter  and enforcing the right controls at the right boundaries is what keeps Phoenix reliable even when parts of its environment are working against it (Azambuja et al., 2023).
Five trust boundaries have been identified in the Phoenix architecture. Each is explained below with a clear account of where it sits, what crosses it, why the trust difference matters, and what enforces it.

Trust Boundary 1 — External World → Data Validation
Where it sits: Between all external data sources and the Phoenix internal pipeline.
What crosses it: Disaster event feeds, cyber threat intelligence, scam reports, and social media data arriving from external APIs and open-source sources.
The trust difference: Everything outside this boundary is untrusted by default. No external source however authoritative it appears that can be assumed to be accurate, authentic, or uncompromised at any given moment. The moment data is allowed past this boundary, the system begins treating it as something it can work with. That shift from untrusted to trusted must be earned through verification, not assumed through familiarity (Chakraborty et al., 2024).
What enforces it: The Data Validation component stands as the gatekeeper here. Schema validation, format checking, source authentication through API keys and digital certificates, and deduplication are all applied before any data proceeds. If data cannot be fetched at all, a Failure to Fetch flag is raised and the retry mechanism activates rather than allowing the pipeline to move forward on incomplete information. Nothing crosses this boundary without being checked.
Why it matters: This is the widest and most consequential trust boundary in Phoenix. Every piece of data the system ever acts on enters here first. A failure at this point a spoofed feed accepted as legitimate, a poisoned dataset passed through unchecked would send corrupted information through every downstream component. This boundary has to be right because everything else depends on it (Salley et al., 2023).

Trust Boundary 2 — Stream Processing → Internal Databases (Scam DB / Cyber Threat DB)
Where it sits: Between the Stream Processing layer and the Scam DB and Cyber Threat DB.
What crosses it: Validated, structured data being written into the internal databases for storage and future retrieval.
The trust difference: Data that has cleared the first boundary has earned a degree of trust it has been authenticated and structurally verified. But writing data into a database introduces a new risk. A database that holds corrupted or manipulated records does not just affect one processing cycle it affects every future AI model classification and every Correlation Engine risk assessment that draws on those records (Li et al., 2022). The databases are long-term assets, and what goes into them shapes how Phoenix behaves for as long as it operates.
What enforces it: Input sanitisation before database writes protects against injection attacks that could corrupt stored records or expose database contents. Write access is restricted to authorised internal pipeline components only no external source can write directly to either database without passing through the validated pipeline first. Data integrity checks confirm that records being written match expected formats before they are committed.
Why it matters: The databases are Phoenix's memory. They hold the historical scam records, threat indicators, and classified AI outputs that the system draws on every time it analyses new content. Corrupted memory produces corrupted judgements and in a system making decisions that affect public safety during disasters, corrupted judgements carry real consequences (Li et al., 2022; Huang & Xiao, 2024).

Trust Boundary 3 — AI Model Processing → Correlation Engine
Where it sits: Between the AI Model Processing component and the Correlation Engine.
What crosses it: Multi-dimensional threat profiles threat classification labels, scam detection results, sentiment scores, and urgency flags generated by the AI models for each piece of processed content.
The trust difference: The AI models are powerful, but their outputs are probabilistic. Every classification comes with a confidence score reflecting how certain the model is. Passing a low-confidence output to the Correlation Engine without flagging its uncertainty would be the same as treating a guess as a fact (Qian et al., 2025). The Correlation Engine needs to know not just what the AI model concluded, but how confident it was because that confidence is a critical input to the risk scoring process that determines how every threat is handled.
What enforces it: Confidence threshold validation ensures that outputs falling below a defined certainty level are flagged before being passed forward, triggering additional scrutiny rather than being accepted at face value. Adversarial input detection monitors for outputs that deviate significantly from expected patterns a potential sign that the model has been fed crafted content designed to manipulate its classifications. Output validation confirms that threat profiles conform to expected structures before the Correlation Engine acts on them.
Why it matters: The Correlation Engine is where threat profiles become risk scores and risk scores become routing decisions. A manipulated or low-confidence AI output accepted without question at this boundary could cause a genuine threat to be under-scored and missed, or a false positive to be over-escalated unnecessarily. Both outcomes undermine the reliability Phoenix exists to provide (Azambuja et al., 2023; Qian et al., 2025).

Trust Boundary 4 — DMT Login → Internal System Functions
Where it sits: Between the public-facing DMT login portal and the privileged internal functions of the Phoenix platform.
What crosses it: Authenticated user sessions granting access to threat review, alert approval, model configuration, and system administration functions.
The trust difference: The DMT login portal is accessible from outside the system it has to be. The functions behind it are not. Reviewing critical threat cases, approving public alerts, configuring AI model parameters, and managing data sources are all actions with direct real-world consequences. This boundary separates what anyone can attempt from what only verified and authorised personnel can do (Spyros et al., 2025) and it is one of the most important lines in the entire Phoenix architecture.
What enforces it: Multi-factor authentication is the primary control, ensuring a stolen password alone is not enough to cross this boundary. Role-Based Access Control limits what each authenticated user can see and do once inside — containing the damage if any single account is compromised. Session management controls including timeout policies and token expiry prevent authenticated sessions from being left open indefinitely or reused after they end. Anomalous login detection monitors for unusual access patterns and raises alerts when something does not look right.
Why it matters: A breach at this boundary does not just compromise data it compromises agency. An adversary who crosses this line gains the ability to act as Phoenix making decisions that the system and the public will treat as legitimate. During an active disaster, that is an extraordinarily dangerous position to be in (Azambuja et al., 2023; Spyros et al., 2025). Protecting this boundary with multiple overlapping controls is not overcaution. It is proportionate to what is at stake.

Trust Boundary 5 — Alert System → Public Communication Channels
Where it sits: Between the internal Phoenix Alert System and external public-facing communication channels SMS gateways, email servers, Firebase push services, and platforms such as VicEmergency.
What crosses it: Verified alert messages carrying official threat intelligence and emergency notifications to communities, emergency services, and government authorities.
The trust difference: Inside Phoenix, an alert is a data object a structured message that has been reviewed, approved, and queued for delivery. The moment it crosses this boundary and enters a public communication channel, it becomes something entirely different. It becomes an official statement received by people who are frightened, who are making real decisions based on what they read, and who have no practical way of knowing whether it is genuine. The trust that communities place in official emergency alerts is not a technical property it is a social contract (Nguyen & Bogle, 2020). And this boundary is where Phoenix either honours that contract or breaks it.
What enforces it: All outbound communications are authenticated using OAuth 2.0 to verify that delivery commands originate from legitimate Phoenix components, and encrypted using TLS 1.3 to prevent interception or alteration in transit. An immutable audit log records every alert action what was sent, when, to whom, and by which component creating a permanent accountability trail. Automatic retry logic with exponential backoff ensures delivery failures during network degradation do not silently prevent alerts from reaching recipients. Rate limiting prevents replay attacks from triggering mass duplicate alert delivery.
Why it matters: Public trust in official emergency alerts takes years to build and moments to destroy. A single successful attack at this boundary a suppressed warning, a false alert, or a spoofed message does not just cause immediate harm. It plants doubt in the minds of communities about whether official alerts can be trusted at all, making every future warning less effective and every future disaster more dangerous (Nguyen & Bogle, 2020; Chirgwin, 2023). Protecting this boundary is not just a technical obligation. It is a civic one.

