Stream Processing → Databases	Validated data stream and internal databases	Input sanitisation, restricted write access, integrity checks
AI Model → Correlation Engine	AI model outputs and risk scoring	Confidence threshold validation, adversarial input detection
DMT Login → Internal Functions	Public login portal and privileged system functions	MFA, RBAC, session management, anomalous login detection
Alert System → Public Channels	Internal alert components and public communication	OAuth 2.0, TLS 1.3, audit logging, retry logic, rate limiting

Why Trust Boundaries Matter for Phoenix
Entry points tell Phoenix where to watch. Trust boundaries tell it what to believe. Together they define the security posture of the entire system not as a collection of isolated controls, but as a coherent layered defence where every part of the architecture knows exactly how much trust to extend to what arrives from outside it.
Each boundary identified here sits at a point where misplaced trust carries significant consequences where accepting something that should have been questioned could corrupt a database, manipulate a risk score, grant unauthorised access, or undermine the integrity of a public alert. Phoenix has been built with the understanding that trust in a security context is not a feeling. It is a decision one that must be earned at every boundary, enforced consistently, and never taken for granted (Azambuja et al., 2023; Chakraborty et al., 2024).

